23:05:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s an excellent point, and you’re absolutely correct that many ancient cities were *affected* by earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, floods, or wars—events that certainly could destroy buildings. So how do we end up with intact brick walls after those disasters? The answer lies in two things: **the difference between destruction and total obliteration**, and the fact that **catastrophic burial is only one of many ways a city gets covered**. Let’s break down your very logical assumption. --- ### 1. “Covered by a disaster” does not always mean “pulverized instantly” When we picture an earthquake, we imagine everything crashing down. And yes, many upper stories, roofs, and weak walls collapsed. But: - **Earthquake damage is often partial.** A quake might bring down roofs and the tops of walls, but the lower portions of well-bonded brick structures, especially if they were already filled with debris or earth from earlier phases, often survived. The fallen rubble (roof beams, mudbrick rubble, plaster) then blankets the floor levels and can actually protect them. That rubble layer acts as the first stage of burial, cushioning and sealing what’s below. - **Seismic vibration can shake things apart, but not make them vanish.** A brick wall that cracks and shears might slump into place rather than disintegrate into dust. It remains as a recognisable wall, just broken. When later sediment or rebuilding buries it, those broken pieces are held in place. A good example is **Knossos on Crete**, which was hit by earthquakes and fires, yet excavators found complete wall paintings and storage jars in the lower rooms, sealed by collapsed ceilings. The disaster caused destruction, but the debris that resulted was the very material that preserved the remaining structures. ### 2. Volcanic destruction: it’s not all lava Volcanoes can indeed incinerate, but the famous “intact” sites like Pompeii or Akrotiri on Santorini were not overwhelmed by lava. They were buried by **tephra** (ash and pumice) and, in Pompeii’s case, later pyroclastic surges. - The initial phase was a fall of lightweight pumice that covered everything like deep, soft snow, filling rooms from the inside and supporting roofs before they could collapse. - The weight of the ash could break roofs, but by then the rooms were already filled with pumice, so the floors and delicate frescos on lower walls were supported and protected. The final surge simply sealed the whole town. - The same happened at **Akrotiri**: It was buried in Theran pumice before any massive collapse, so three-story houses with intact windows and painted walls were preserved. The city wasn’t crushed to powder; it was *encapsulated*. So even a violent volcanic disaster can deposit a protective, low-impact blanket if the eruption style is right. ### 3. Many ancient cities were never hit by a single catastrophic event at all This is the biggest misunderstanding. Your idea that they were “covered by natural disasters” applies to some famous sites, but the vast majority of buried ancient cities—especially the tell mounds of Mesopotamia, the Indus Valley, and the mound cities of Anatolia—were buried by **slow, mundane processes**: - **Abandonment and drifting sand/silt.** They were simply deserted (due to climate change, river shifting, political collapse) and then over decades and centuries, wind and occasional floods deposited material inside buildings and over them. No dramatic Hollywood event, just gradual accumulation. - **Mud decay and “tellification.”** Mudbrick walls, when abandoned and exposed to rain, melt into a thick clay sludge. That sludge washes into rooms and streets, raising the ground level. New builders then built on top of the compacted sludge. This repeated over millennia, turning a city into a layered mound, with the oldest buildings at the bottom, buried in their own decayed substance. This is a *self-burial* process that’s actually quite gentle because the disintegrating