no water erosion, no roots). In short, your idea that earthquakes and volcanic eruptions destroy everything is right for the parts that receive the direct kinetic force—like a roof being shaken to bits. But the parts that survive the initial event, even if they’re just 1-2 meters high, become the “intact brick structures” we unearth, preserved by the very rubble and soil that buried them. It’s a two-step process: partial destruction, then burial that turns the survivors into a fossil.
